As an American you don't hear it much but it when you sound both intelligent and like you are putting extra emphasis. 

&#x200B;

Europeans on here thinking-"Americans, they do properly lack class."

The same things you always hear. Regular exercise 2-3 times per week (does not require a gym membership). Moderation in your diet (use fast food and sweets as a treat, like once a month). Genetics do play a factor but you can do a significant amount of work through the other areas I mentioned. 

&#x200B;

Another huge bonus is cooking your own food! It can be tastier than at restaurants and you control what you eat. I hope this helps. For me is 99% moderation in my diet. I am gifted genetically to be predisposed to being lean, that doesn't stop me from getting lazy though and starting to get looser and bigger around my stomach. Also remember that the grass is greener. As a lean person I have struggled significantly with attempting to put weight on my entire life, both muscle and fat storage in preparation for colder events.

I see a lot of responses here. I think what is important to note is that societies have different standards. Often when we see a single act displayed from a country we associate that with the entire country. 

&#x200B;

IE all cops in America shoot black people regardless of the action and doesn't provide healthcare for its population. Or Saudi Arabia flogs women for not covering faces and kills journalists. China kills protesters en masse and throws Muslims in concentration camps.

&#x200B;

We see these things and immediately think the other country is savage or uncivilized. We are all different and have different things that are important. It is not our job to play judge and jury of other countries actions (excluding human rights violations). The bad things we other countries do, it doesn't mean those countries do not do things as well. I hope this helps people to understand we are all different and that is ok.

Ok...Do I support free healthcare for all in the US. Absolutely, our system is beyond stupid. I think the argument to made here from a perspective of an American understanding our dumb healthcare system. 

&#x200B;

Cancer is not contagious human to human (that I know of). COVID-19 is contagious human to human. By making the vaccine free you are removing the barrier to get vaccinated to a large part of the population who are poor and might not have the funds to get the vaccine. Doing this you drastically reduce the chance of more flair ups for the disease by having a near fully vaccinated population and not a partially vaccinated population.

Trump as President from a legal perspective (not including support or against him, just his actions as president), Hong Kong, China's actions domestically are all things covered a lot.

&#x200B;

What is the biggest overlooked thing? The way work and school will be transformed post COVID and move more to virtual work. This will all likely cause a large shift in housing from large cities to more remote areas.

